        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Promix Use Stevia or Artificial Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No: Promix protein powders use no stevia, sucralose, monk fruit or sugar alcohols, per the brand. The unflavored whey, One Ingredient Whey and unflavored isolate are unsweetened. The flavored concentrates use maple sugar, and the flavored isolates use organic coconut sugar, which Promix says replaces stevia, sucralose, gums and sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the clearest difference between Promix and most flavored wheys. A typical tub of chocolate whey is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, or with stevia and monk fruit in the natural brands. Some people dislike the aftertaste of those sweeteners, and some avoid them on principle. Promix takes the other route and uses a small amount of real sugar instead, which is why it shows up in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guides.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade is a few grams of added sugar. Vanilla Whey carries 4 g of added sugar per serving and the flavored isolates carry 5 g, per Promix. For most people that is a small amount, about one teaspoon, but it adds up if you drink two shakes a day, and it matters if you track sugar for diabetes or another medical reason. In that case the unflavored bags, mixed into something you already eat, are the better fit, and it is worth asking your doctor or a registered dietitian how a daily shake fits your plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored versus flavored is also a cost question. Sugar and cocoa take up part of every scoop, so the flavored bags hold fewer servings: 31 for Vanilla Whey against about 37 for the unflavored, by Promix's own per-serving prices. If you plan to blend the powder into a smoothie anyway, the unflavored bag gives you more protein per dollar and lets you sweeten to taste. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide compares Promix with the other plain options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Unflavored for value, flavored for drinking with water</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick an unflavored Promix bag if the powder goes into coffee, oats, smoothies or baking. Pick Vanilla Whey or a flavored isolate only if you drink it with water or milk alone, and count the 4 to 5 g of added sugar.</p>
