        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Promix, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Promix suits buyers who want a grass-fed whey with no stevia or sucralose and are willing to pay a premium for a short label. It does not suit drug-tested athletes or anyone shopping purely on price per gram.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Promix to buy</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Unflavored Promix Whey</strong> is the best value and the most flexible: two ingredients, 25 g of protein and about $1.73 per 25 g on Amazon, less in the 5 lb bag. <strong>Promix Whey Isolate</strong> is for anyone sensitive to lactose or chasing protein per scoop, at 30 g a serving. <strong>Vanilla Whey</strong> is for people who drink shakes with water and want real sugar instead of sweeteners. <strong>One Ingredient Whey</strong> is for purists who want literally nothing but whey and will use a blender.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Promix Vegan</strong> is the pick for dairy-free buyers who like the brand's approach, at about $1.53 per 25 g. It is a pea protein isolate, so it competes with single-ingredient pea powders; our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide shows several cheaper options with outside certifications. Promix's casein and beef protein isolate are niche products that are best compared in our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein</a> and <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein</a> guides.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who should skip Promix</h3>

          <p>
            Skip Promix if you compete in a drug-tested sport, because it has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification. Skip it if you want the cheapest protein, since standard whey blends cost far less per gram. And skip the flavored bags if you track added sugar closely. Anyone with kidney disease, a milk allergy, or who is pregnant or on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding protein powder, and our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps set a sensible daily target.
          </p>
        </div>

