          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Promix protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, by its own account. Promix says every product is third-party tested by Eurofins, and you can enter your bag's lot code on its lab results page to see the record. It publishes limits such as lead under 5 ppb. It does not claim NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice, so it is not screened for banned substances in the way tested athletes need.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Promix protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Promix has some of the shortest labels in the category: two ingredients in the unflavored whey, one in One Ingredient Whey, and four in Vanilla Whey, with no gums, stevia or sucralose. Clean has no legal definition, though. The flavored bags add 4 to 5 g of sugar, and anyone with kidney disease, allergies or pregnancy should ask a doctor first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Promix contain lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Promix whey concentrate contains lactose, as all whey concentrates do, though Promix does not state the amount. Promix Whey Isolate is micro-filtered to under 1 g of lactose per serving, per Promix, which most people with lactose intolerance tolerate. Promix Vegan is dairy-free. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid both wheys entirely, because the allergen is the milk protein itself.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Promix use stevia or artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Promix says its powders contain no stevia, sucralose or sugar alcohols. The unflavored whey, One Ingredient Whey and unflavored isolate are unsweetened. Vanilla Whey uses maple sugar, with 4 g added per serving, and the flavored isolates use organic coconut sugar, with 5 g added per serving, per Promix at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Promix flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We have not tasted Promix, so we cannot rank flavors ourselves. Vanilla and chocolate are the core flavors across the whey, isolate and vegan lines, and the unflavored bags are the most versatile for cooking and smoothies. The isolate adds Cookies and Cream, Strawberry Milk and Banana Cream. Expect all of them to be less sweet than mainstream whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about Promix?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The predictable complaints follow from the label: unflavored Promix tastes like plain dairy, One Ingredient Whey needs a blender because it has no lecithin, and the flavored bags are milder than sucralose-sweetened shakes. Price is the other one. At about $1.73 to $2.14 per 25 g on Amazon, Promix costs more than mainstream whey blends.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Promix whey isolate better than Promix whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is better for lactose-sensitive buyers and anyone who wants more protein per scoop: 30 g against 25 g, with under 1 g of lactose, per Promix. It is worse on price, at about $2.11 per 25 g against about $1.73 for the unflavored concentrate at the Amazon prices we saw. If dairy suits you, the concentrate is the better buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

